b/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂r}=-\\frac{∂Q}{∂s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298511} {:answer/id #uuid "fb085e36-cca2-5830-b0f4-54f7117d6aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is exact" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb085e36-cca2-5830-b0f4-54f7117d6aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is exact" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298622} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2d4c5a6-7416-5423-8123-c99047af638c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a8eaa91-b14a-5a35-aac6-2b6c5a891db8" :question/math-academy-id "q-318601" :question/problem "Determine whether the following differential equation is exact:\n\n$$\n(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nA differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(r,s)\\,\\text{d}r+Q(r,s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it satisfies the condition\n\n$$\n\\,{{field-1}},\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP=\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\n$$\nQ=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\,{{field-4}},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂r}=\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\,{{field-6}},\n$$\n\n the equation {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(r,s)\\,\\text{d}r+Q(r,s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂r}.\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP(r,s)=1-s{e}^{rs},\\,Q(r,s)=-r{e}^{rs}+4s.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂}{∂s}(1-s{e}^{rs}) \\\\  & =-\\,({e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂}{∂r}(-r{e}^{rs}+4s) \\\\  & =-\\,({e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂r},\n$$\n\nthe exactness condition is satisfied. Therefore, the given differential equation $\\text{is exact}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daff356a-05c7-5413-b4a9-22dd8c231ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465939} {:answer/id #uuid "494c2be5-029b-57ad-a2e0-c99b1d96fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daff356a-05c7-5413-b4a9-22dd8c231ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465939} :answer-field/id #uuid "3202f018-29ed-500d-959e-76c6cab125b7" :answer-field/key "$({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81a1850a-0ff0-5a27-a2df-03b614277b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465941} {:answer/id #uuid "73fb1274-128f-5ad3-9378-062ea505cca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a1850a-0ff0-5a27-a2df-03b614277b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465941} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a399a13-687a-5578-9da2-e8e60a8d3d1f" :answer-field/key "$(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1764fb8b-5951-50f4-a435-a85c42961dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465943} {:answer/id #uuid "09e071b8-8e60-56c8-8e8b-b575e492b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e071b8-8e60-56c8-8e8b-b575e492b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465944} :answer-field/id #uuid "795091ec-54dd-5546-a4f6-371522d69932" :answer-field/key "$(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70dbdf12-3bc6-5349-9efa-62768bd9b3d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318629" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}+y,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+2) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n \nThus, equation $({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is not exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3xy,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy) \\\\  & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation $(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-4x)=4x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=6y-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, equation $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991c977e-b45f-5410-8eda-0f8842dc0de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465945} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea73e7b-280f-5c50-bec6-7d04449a14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea73e7b-280f-5c50-bec6-7d04449a14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465946} :answer-field/id #uuid "66130991-4adc-539d-8177-a4a5bd39e0ec" :answer-field/key "$(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f50423-0ec2-57d0-ab9f-849858f31c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465947} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f97384-e308-5af2-9c38-2575f722e764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f50423-0ec2-57d0-ab9f-849858f31c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465947} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8b3d88-bb0e-5d89-adac-eb84c79e516b" :answer-field/key "$({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8962ecd-3ce2-541f-9931-765f1942f131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465949} {:answer/id #uuid "28d3123a-f2ff-579d-bd7c-5378c5ea42b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8962ecd-3ce2-541f-9931-765f1942f131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465949} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5f83ce-fbbe-585e-98c9-f0216caf3a08" :answer-field/key "$(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "861223b8-6640-50cd-ba1a-46920dc4bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-318630" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy+5,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+5) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+7) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{2}+x,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation $({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-2x)=2x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y-3x) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation $(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ is not exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bac31f3f-82dd-5744-9c52-62141c569e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+\\operatorname{sin}(x)){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bac31f3f-82dd-5744-9c52-62141c569e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+\\operatorname{sin}(x)){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d13588f-071e-5a52-a869-877b2c497c93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9a55990-04ba-5f4c-bd0b-a335f0aeb15a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319350" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)).$\n\n$({e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=2+sin⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}sec⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2+sin⁡(x))+({e}^{x})(cos⁡(x)) \\\\  & =(2+sin⁡(x)){e}^{x}+{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70915bcc-86df-54d2-a4fe-50e0b9f25633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70915bcc-86df-54d2-a4fe-50e0b9f25633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298034} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c11172-6006-5675-abeb-a1bc58910b0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dca45644-472a-517e-a9dd-987f8f5907bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-319357" :question/problem "Differentiate $2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x).$\n\n$(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡(x)+2{x}^{2}\\cdot (cos⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(4x)(cos⁡(x))+(2{x}^{2})(-sin⁡(x)) \\\\  & =4xcos⁡(x)-2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284d504d-e400-57ba-866e-f8dff66216fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284d504d-e400-57ba-866e-f8dff66216fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297724} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb4d22f-ab7d-5aa3-8db2-cf3a5509f8ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9df2a3ce-ad57-5588-83d2-42b69ca3314d" :question/math-academy-id "q-319385" :question/problem "Differentiate $\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x).$\n\n$(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′}=\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡(x)+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (tan⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}(tan⁡(x))+(\\sqrt{x})({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd6401f2-6da7-5c65-8aa1-add3b78f3859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6401f2-6da7-5c65-8aa1-add3b78f3859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298448} :answer-field/id #uuid "26876702-3fb0-5366-a888-d8bd68fa6992" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ccc1718-8889-5c4c-8f82-a3c2895dd7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-319425" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}tan⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{(tan⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}ln⁡(4))tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fe4615-3627-5184-b596-faacf4579fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94fe4615-3627-5184-b596-faacf4579fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5b5855-e173-5cc0-be78-e8c7ed94613e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6dd1a867-f879-56d0-b4c0-d0297b5035d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319806" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x+2)cos⁡x,$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x+2)\\cdot (-sin⁡x)+cos⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-(x+2)sin⁡x+cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)sin⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+cos⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)(1)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a9ff769-7920-54d9-9d56-bc88c33849af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a9ff769-7920-54d9-9d56-bc88c33849af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298084} :answer-field/id #uuid "4571a1bf-33ea-5e4c-ab47-61e0c018609f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6b71a24-90f7-5ca7-b521-491ff5b33ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319829" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)ln⁡(4x-3),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=ln⁡(4x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4x-3}+ln⁡(4x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4(1)-3}+ln⁡(4(1)-3) \\\\  & =0+ln⁡(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"}]}
